                <pb facs="0998" n="886" xml:id="pg886"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.6363 Manchuria/153: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-03-25T17:00:00+09:00">March 25, 1935—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 25—9:54 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>63. Department’s 44, March 22, 6 p.m.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>The British Embassy today received a reply from the Foreign Office to its
                        formal representations of November 24 on the subject of the oil monopoly in
                        Manchuria. The reply restates the Japanese stand as communicated previously
                        to the American and British Embassies and further deals with Manchukuo’s
                        alleged non-responsibilities under the treaties between China and other
                        countries. While the reply has not yet been translated it seems to be
                        unconciliatory in tone.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>This Embassy has not yet received its reply although it is momentarily
                        expected. When received and translated we shall telegraph the text or a
                        digest thereof with our suggestions as to further steps.</item>
                </list>
                <p>Repeated to Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
